          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, with emphasis on hybrid retrieval as the production default.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-15T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Well-articulated description of hybrid retrieval from a technical source; general pattern confirmed in practice but awaiting additional corroboration.\\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines **sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25) with **dense vector search**, then merges the two ranked result sets to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Merging Strategies\\n\\nThe most common merging approach is **reciprocal rank fusion** (RRF), which combines rankings from both methods to produce a unified result set.\\n\\n### Performance Characteristics\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n- **Rare identifiers** (error codes, product SKUs) \u2014 where lexical search excels\\n- **Natural language intent** \u2014 where semantic search excels\\n\\nThis makes it especially valuable for real-world queries that do not neatly separate these concerns.\\n\\n### Production Adoption\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, reflecting its robustness across diverse query types.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Evaluation source on retrieval patterns and hybrid approaches (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-09)\\n\u0022}"
        }
